-- Passage rg9-text-structure-p01
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('9e9597aa-7437-5690-87a5-9eb487fca2ec', 'rg9-text-structure-p01', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g9-text-structure'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Brine Before the Storm$q$, $q$For decades, road crews in the hill town of Ostrava Bend waited for snow to fall before they did anything about it. Once a few inches had piled up, trucks rolled out and spread rock salt across the pavement. The method worked, but slowly. Salt must dissolve before it can melt ice, and on very cold mornings that took a long time. Meanwhile, the first layer of snow had already been pressed into slick ice by passing cars.

In 2019, the town's road supervisor, Marisol Achterberg, tried a different approach. Instead of waiting, her crews sprayed the roads with brine, a mixture of salt and water, a day before a storm was expected. The brine dried into a thin white film. When snow began to fall, the salt in the film was already in place, so the first flakes melted on contact and could not bond to the road.

The results were clear by the end of that first winter. The town used about a third less salt than in the year before, because brine spreads evenly instead of bouncing into ditches the way dry crystals do. Plows also cleared the roads faster, since the snow lifted away from the pavement instead of sticking to it.

The method has limits. Rain before a storm can wash the brine away, and in extremely cold weather even brine freezes. On those days, crews still turn to rock salt and sand. Achterberg calls brine a first line of defense rather than a complete answer.

Still, the change has spread. Four neighboring towns now pretreat their main roads, and Ostrava Bend has built a small plant to mix its own brine. The roads are not perfect on snowy mornings, but they are safer and cheaper to keep clear than they were.$q$, 'draft')
