-- Passage rg9-text-structure-p02
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('fa67e6a3-5aa5-5099-a113-ffad0483bc56', 'rg9-text-structure-p02', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g9-text-structure'), null,
        '9-12', $q$The Library That Floated$q$, $q$In the early 1900s, families who farmed along the Calder River lived miles from the nearest town. Roads were rough and often flooded in spring, so a trip to buy supplies could take a full day. Books were rare in these homes, and children who finished the few volumes their families owned had nothing new to read.

In 1912, a schoolteacher named Adaeze Morrow proposed an unusual solution. If readers could not reach the library, she argued, the library could travel to the readers along the one route that every farm shared: the river. With donations from the town of Pellham, she bought an old cargo barge, built shelves along its walls, and stocked them with about six hundred books.

The first trips were slow. The barge drifted downstream for two weeks, stopping at farm landings where Morrow rang a bell to announce her arrival. Families borrowed books and returned them on the next visit, a month later. By 1915, the barge carried more than two thousand books, and a second librarian had joined the crew.

The floating library also faced real setbacks. A flood in 1918 tore the barge from its moorings and soaked nearly half the collection. Volunteers dried pages on clotheslines for weeks, and readers in Pellham donated replacements.

As paved roads reached the valley in the 1930s, a motor truck called a bookmobile took over the route, and the barge was retired in 1937. Today it sits on blocks outside the Pellham library, repainted in its original green. A sign beside it lists the river landings it once served, a reminder that a library is defined less by its building than by its readers.$q$, 'draft')
