-- Passage rg9-text-structure-p03
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('11b4be08-5082-5859-b6de-fe44c91d3f3a', 'rg9-text-structure-p03', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g9-text-structure'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Twenty-Four Seconds$q$, $q$In its first seasons, the Westmark Basketball League had no time limit on possessions. A team that took an early lead could simply pass the ball around the court for minutes at a time, and the other team could do little except foul. Some games ended with scores in the teens. Attendance fell sharply, from an average of four thousand fans per game in 1949 to fewer than two thousand by 1953.

The league's owners met that winter to look for a cause. Several blamed ticket prices, and others blamed the new television sets that kept people at home. A team owner named Viktor Halloran disagreed. He had charted every possession in a dozen games and found that the slowest games drew the smallest crowds in the following weeks. Fans, he concluded, were staying away because the games had become dull to watch.

Halloran proposed a simple rule: a team would have twenty-four seconds to attempt a shot, or it would lose the ball. He arrived at the number by dividing the length of a game by the number of shots he believed a lively game should contain. Many owners doubted it would work, but they agreed to test the rule during the preseason.

The effects were immediate. Scores in the test games rose by more than twenty points, and teams could no longer protect a lead by stalling. Players had to run, pass quickly and shoot with confidence. When the rule became official in 1954, attendance climbed back above three thousand within two seasons.

The shot clock did not solve every problem, since ticket prices remained high and television kept growing. Yet it changed the pace of the sport so completely that later fans could hardly imagine a game without it.$q$, 'draft')
