-- Passage rg9-text-structure-p05
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('4f66f553-620b-58fc-b3cc-45d59ae92695', 'rg9-text-structure-p05', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g9-text-structure'), null,
        '9-12', $q$The Right Rain at the Right Time$q$, $q$Every few years, the dry hills of the Sarvo Basin turn bright orange and purple almost overnight. Millions of wildflowers open at once, and visitors drive for hours to see them. For a long time, local guides gave a simple explanation for these displays, which they called super blooms: a wet winter brings a flowering spring.

That explanation is partly true. The seeds of many desert flowers can rest in the soil for years, waiting for enough moisture to sprout. In a dry winter they stay buried, and in a rainy one they come to life. But botanist Leilani Castellanos noticed that some very wet winters produced only scattered flowers, while one with average rainfall produced the brightest bloom in decades.

To find out why, Castellanos and her students studied twenty years of weather records alongside photographs of each spring. They found that the timing of the rain mattered as much as the amount. Seeds needed a soaking storm in late autumn to start growing, followed by gentle rains spread through the winter. A single huge storm in February did little, because the seedlings that sprouted dried out before they could flower.

Temperature played a role too. Cold nights in early winter slowed the growth of weeds that would otherwise crowd out the wildflowers. In warmer years, the weeds took over first.

So the guides' old rule needs an update. A super bloom does depend on rain, but it depends on the right rain at the right time, along with cool nights that hold back the competition. Castellanos now posts a bloom forecast each January, and she has been right about the last four springs.$q$, 'draft')
