-- Passage rg9-text-structure-p06
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('a9ca3bf5-1046-59c6-8af6-82b7ada1221d', 'rg9-text-structure-p06', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g9-text-structure'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Two Ways to Light a Stage$q$, $q$When the Larkspur Community Theater replaced its stage lights last year, its lighting designer, Tomasz Adeyemi, had to choose between two very different systems. Each one can fill a stage with color, but they do it in opposite ways, and the choice affects everything from the budget to how a show is rehearsed.

The older system uses bright white lamps and colored filters called gels. A gel is a thin sheet of tinted plastic slid into a frame on the front of a light. To change a color, a technician climbs a ladder and swaps the sheet. Gel lights are inexpensive to buy, and many designers love the warm, soft quality of their light. However, the lamps run hot, use a great deal of electricity, and slowly fade the gels, which must be replaced every few weeks.

The newer system uses fixtures packed with small red, green, blue and white lights. By mixing these in different amounts, a single fixture can produce thousands of colors, and a designer can change them from a computer at the back of the theater. These fixtures cost several times as much as gel lights, but they use far less power and stay cool.

For Adeyemi, the deciding difference was time. With gel lights, trying a new color during rehearsal meant stopping while someone climbed a ladder. With the newer fixtures, he could test ten colors in a minute while the actors kept working.

In the end, Larkspur chose a mix. Newer fixtures now hang over the main acting area, where colors change often, while a row of gel lights remains at the front of the stage to give actors' faces the warm glow that audiences have come to expect.$q$, 'draft')
